---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj#article',
  headline: 'exFAT vs FAT32 for CDJs - Which Format Actually Works',
  description: 'Which format works on CDJ-2000, CDJ-3000 and XDJ players? The compatibility table, the 4GB myth, and the one setup that works everywhere.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  // ARTICLE IMAGE (2026-08-04). Google's Article guidance asks for an image,
  // and without one an Article node is never a candidate for a rich result.
  // This is the article's own share card, generated from its own title by
  // scripts/gen-og.mjs, so it represents this article and nothing else.
  // mainEntityOfPage is an @id reference rather than a bare URL string so it
  // resolves to the WebPage node Base.astro emits, instead of being a URL
  // that merely looks like one.
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj.png',
};
---
<Base
  title="exFAT vs FAT32 for CDJ USB Drives | Save My Gig"
  description="Which format works on CDJ-2000, CDJ-3000 and XDJ players? The compatibility table, the 4GB myth, and the one setup that works everywhere."
  path="/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj"
  crumbName="exFAT vs FAT32"
  ogImage="/og/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line">Format_Question</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">exFAT vs <span class="accent">FAT32</span>:<br>WHICH ONE FOR CDJs?</h1>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>The answer that never fails:</strong> FAT32 with an MBR partition map plays on every rekordbox-compatible player ever made. exFAT works only on newer hardware. If you play in booths you don't control, FAT32 is the professional choice.</p>
    </div>

        {/* FIRST SECTION OPEN ON ARRIVAL (H5, 2026-08-05). The h1 above says
            "DO THIS, IN THIS ORDER" and this page used to answer it with three
            closed rows: zero instructions on screen for someone who is already
            panicking. The first row is the fastest checks, which is what
            everyone needs first, so it is server-rendered open. The rest stay
            closed, and the exclusive `name` still means opening another closes
            this one. `open` on the element, never JavaScript: it has to be in
            the initial HTML and it has to survive JS being off. */}
        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc" open>
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Compatibility in detail</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32 + MBR</span>, works on CDJ-850, 900, 2000, 2000NXS/NXS2, 3000, every XDJ. The universal key.</li>
      <!-- Scope corrected against AlphaTheta's official exFAT FAQ: it names
           CDJ-3000, XDJ-RX3 and XDJ-XZ (firmware thresholds in facts.js), and
           the CDJ-3000X and XDJ-AZ product FAQs add both to the list. The old
           line said "CDJ-3000 and recent XDJ firmware", which was both too
           narrow and too vague, and omitted the NXS2. -->
      <li><span class="mono" translate="no">exFAT</span>, supported on CDJ-3000, CDJ-3000X, CDJ-1500X, XDJ-RX3, XDJ-XZ and XDJ-AZ (firmware matters, see the <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/rekordbox#which-player">player table</a>); <strong>refused by every CDJ-2000, including the NXS2</strong>, still common in clubs worldwide. One trap almost nobody mentions: rekordbox writes exFAT to <strong>USB drives only</strong>. It will not export to an exFAT <strong>SD card</strong> at all, whatever the player supports.</li>
      <li><span class="mono" translate="no">NTFS / APFS</span>, never. AlphaTheta's own rekordbox manual lists NTFS as unsupported on both SD cards and USB drives, so rekordbox will not export to it in the first place.</li>
      <li><span class="mono" translate="no">Partition scheme</span>, choose <span class="mono" translate="no">MBR</span>. AlphaTheta documents this for specific models: their support articles state that the CDJ-3000 (article 4406135001625) and the XDJ-AN (article 53214453591009) do not support the GUID Partition Map. For the rest of the range the manual is silent on schemes, so MBR is the manufacturer's documented answer where they name one and our recommendation everywhere else. A drive left on GPT, which is what you get if you erase the volume instead of the device on a Mac, is a recurring cause of "it formatted fine but the player refuses it". MBR costs you nothing.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">"But FAT32 has a 4GB limit!"</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p>Per file, not per drive, and it is the one limit that matters. No DJ track approaches 4GB; even a 30-minute WAV is under 1GB. The only DJs who need exFAT are the ones carrying multi-hour uncompressed recordings on their gig drive, which is its own mistake. FAT32 has other ceilings, a 2TB volume limit and the per-model file and folder counts AlphaTheta publishes in support article 4406512510105, but on a 32 to 64GB music drive none of them come up.</p>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The trap: how drives ship</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Most drives above 32GB ship <strong>exFAT from the factory</strong>, so a brand-new "professional" 64GB stick fails on a CDJ-2000 out of the box.</li>
      <li>Windows won't offer FAT32 for large drives in the Format dialog; on Mac, choose <span class="mono" translate="no">MS-DOS (FAT)</span> in Disk Utility with <span class="mono" translate="no">Master Boot Record</span> - <a href="/fix/format-usb-for-cdj">full walkthrough here</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The rule to remember</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p><strong>Format for the oldest player you might ever face, not the most modern one you own.</strong> The festival main stage has CDJ-3000s; the afterparty basement has a CDJ-2000 with a cigarette burn. FAT32 plays both.</p>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="exfat_vs_fat32" />
  </main>
</Base>
